Explain the anatomical structure and function of the mandible in the human body, including its role in the skeletal system and its relationship with other facial bones.
The mandible, also known as the lower jaw or jawbone, is the largest, strongest, and lowest bone in the human face. It forms the lower jaw and holds the lower teeth in place. The mandible is a curved bone with two surfaces and two borders, and its front part gives structure to the chin. It articulates with the left and right temporal bones at the temporomandibular joints, allowing for movement of the jaw. The mandible is formed from the fusion of the left and right mandibular prominences in the fetus, and the point of fusion, the mandibular symphysis, is still visible as a faint ridge in the midline. The mandible plays a crucial role in the skeletal system, providing support for the lower teeth and facilitating mastication (chewing). Its relationship with other facial bones is essential for maintaining the structure and function of the face.
