What is the historical context of the farthing coin, including its design and production process?
The farthing coin was first minted in England in the 13th century, initially in silver. Early examples are rare, but it is known that the first copper farthings were issued during the reign of King James I. The design of the farthing coin typically featured a cross on the reverse, and it was often similar in design to the penny but smaller in size. In the 13th century, farthings were produced in the short-cross style, with five examples discovered dating back to the reign of King Henry III. These early farthings are thought to be trials rather than regular coins due to the lack of known examples and documentary evidence.
