Explain the genetic basis of sex determination in humans, specifically the role of X and Y chromosomes.
In humans, the genetic basis of sex determination is primarily determined by the presence of the X and Y chromosomes. Females typically have two X chromosomes (XX), while males have one X and one Y chromosome (XY). The presence of the Y chromosome carries genes that influence male development, while the X chromosome carries genes that are present in both males and females. The specific genes on the Y chromosome, such as those responsible for testis development, determine maleness. The X chromosome, on the other hand, carries genes that are present in both males and females, but the presence of an extra X chromosome in females does not determine femaleness. The interaction between the X and Y chromosomes during development determines the sex of the offspring.
