Analyze how the introduction of squash in North America influenced the game's evolution, including any changes in rules, equipment, or playing styles.
The introduction of squash in North America in the 19th century led to its growth in popularity, with various schools, clubs, and private citizens building squash courts. Initially, there were no set dimensions for these courts, but the first squash court in North America appeared at St. Paul's School in Concord, New Hampshire in 1884. This marked the beginning of the game's evolution in the region.

In 1904, the United States Squash Rackets Association (USSRA), now known as U.S. Squash, was formed in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, becoming the earliest national association of squash in the world. This led to the establishment of standards for the game, including the development of official court plans by 1929.

The introduction of squash in North America also led to changes in equipment, with the replacement of "gut" strings with synthetic strings. However, the rules and scoring of the game remained largely similar to those of the original game of squash rackets, which was an offshoot of rackets.

The hardball version of squash, which was popular in North America until the 1980s, had distinct differences in court dimensions and equipment compared to the international version of the game. The hardball game was played on a court with a "tin" at the bottom of the front wall, whereas the international version was played on a court with a different type of front wall.

Overall, the introduction of squash in North America led to its growth in popularity
